<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kletorologion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">899</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Kletorologion of Philotheos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kletorologion of Philotheos is the longest and most important of the Byzantine lists of offices and court precedence, published in September 899 in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-vi-the-wise">Leo VI the Wise</a> by Philotheos, a protospatharios and atriklines otherwise unknown. As atriklines he received the guests at imperial banquets and seated them by their place in the hierarchy, and he compiled the work from older kletorologia as a guide to the order of the banquets and the name and value of each title. It survives only as an appendix in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-ceremoniis">De Ceremoniis</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine Porphyrogennetos</a>. Its four sections survey the ranks and offices of the empire, set the order of precedence and advise on the year's banquets.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rank flowed above all from him: the court awarded titles and offices in a strict order of precedence, set out in lists such as the <b>Kletorologion of Philotheos</b> (899), with separate ranks for eunuchs.</blockquote><a href="/societies/byzantine-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Byzantine Empire</a></li></ul></section>
